# What is evidence, and what is a byproduct.
#
# The rule: git carries the CLAIMS, releases carry the EVIDENCE.
#
#   records/arms.jsonl, records/prereg.jsonl   the claims — every number and
#                                               every prediction. Small, and the
#                                               only thing a reader needs to
#                                               check the argument.
#   versions/current.md, versions/DISCARDED.md the record and the negatives.
#                                               Published negatives are the
#                                               point of the project.
#   state/doctor.json, state/doctor_history.jsonl
#   records/halt_audit.jsonl                   the diagnosis and every halt.
#   state/trajectory.html, state/dashboard_*.html
#                                               the figures, self-contained.
#
# What is deliberately NOT here, and why — each of these was considered:
#
#   records/*/**.items.jsonl   5.4 MB per arm, ~100 MB by the end. This is the
#                              per-question data the adversarial probe A1
#                              recomputes every headline from, so excluding it
#                              makes every number in the repository
#                              unverifiable. It is still excluded: git history
#                              is immutable, so 100 MB is paid by every clone
#                              forever and the repository becomes unusable.
#                              These ship as per-arm GitHub Release assets
#                              instead — immutable, versioned, downloadable at a
#                              stable URL, and carrying a sha256 so the reader
#                              can confirm they got the rows A1 used. See
#                              pipeline/publish.py, which cuts and uploads them.
#
#   corpus/                    17 MB, and fully regenerable: `python
#                              scripts/build_corpus.py --out corpus` prints the
#                              per-file sha256, so the reader can rebuild it and
#                              verify it. Shipping a regenerable artifact is
#                              strictly worse than shipping the command.
#
#   ckpt/                      model weights. Never in git at any project.
#
#   .venv/                     927 MB of dependencies, pinned in config/.
#
#   *.log, logs/               the live tail. The durable record is arms.jsonl
#                              and the version card; a log is a debugging
#                              artifact, and committing one on every arm would
#                              bury every real change in noise.

.venv/
ckpt/
corpus/

# Per-question rows: shipped as release assets, see above.
records/*/*.items.jsonl

# Live logs. The daemon writes here continuously and a commit taken mid-write
# captures a torn line, which is noise at best and a corrupt record at worst.
logs/
*.log

# Transient. The publisher regenerates the snapshot before committing, and
# doctor.json is rewritten every watchdog tick, so committing the pair produces
# a commit every 15 minutes that says nothing.
state/dashboard_snapshot.html
state/dashboard_demo.html
state/doctor.json.tmp
state/halt_logged
records/*.bak

# Nothing machine-specific may be committed. A path like /Users/<someone>/...
# makes a repository unrunnable on every other machine, and the handoff note on
# the upstream project records exactly that failure being hit and then fixed.
# pipeline/paths.py is the one place a machine path is allowed to appear.
*.abs.json
paths.local.json
.DS_Store
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Lean build output. `formal/.lake` is 7.6 GB — the Mathlib oleans — and none of it
# is evidence. `formal/lake-manifest.json` IS committed, because it pins the exact
# Mathlib revision, and a proof whose library version is not recorded cannot be
# re-checked.
formal/.lake/
